  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 227 AM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nThe forecast from Wednesday night through the end of the week is the \nsame as that of today and tomorrow. Widespread 70s increase a degree \nor two each day unless warming is perturbed by high clouds. Late in \nthe week mid-level ridging will be shoved eastward and flattened as \na longwave trough strengthens over the Four Corners region. This \nsystem is forecast to drive a cold front into the Midwest late \nSaturday into Sunday, but latest model guidance indicates the front \nwill pivot into a more west-east orientation before it reaches \nMissouri/central Illinois. This would keep accompanying rain and \ncold air to our north and lengthen the duration of our warm streak. \nEnsembles, aside from the GEFS, appear to support this solution. 850 \nmb temperatures Sunday into Monday are still forecast to be about \n10C - the same as today - so a drastic cooldown within that period \nis not likely if the models are correct. Beyond that guidance \ndiverges, and that's reliant on if the cold front does push through \nthe CWA or not Monday into Tuesday. The NBM high temperature IQRs \nreflect the uncertainty quite well, with KSTL jumping from a 4 \ndegree split on Sunday to a 12 degree split by Tuesday.",
